# 2026-07-30 implementation notes
- Redesign: per-case `jinn_waitq_node_t` waiter nodes (stack-allocated in
  jinn_select, embedded in the coroutine for plain send/recv), so a
  selector sits on EVERY case's wait queue at once (Go's model). All
  wakers claim the selector through one shared claim word (CAS 0→1);
  losers skip the node. Channel wait queues now link through nodes, not
  the coroutine's intrusive `next` (which stays for the scheduler inject
  queue and actor join).
- Park handoff generalized to multi-lock (worker `held_locks[]`): select
  parks holding EVERY case lock; the scheduler releases them in reverse
  acquisition order after the context save. Because re-acquisition
  (lock_all) is forward in sorted order, a woken selector cannot pass its
  first lock — and so cannot touch or reuse the stack-resident node
  array — until the release loop has finished.
- Node/stack lifetime invariant: select-node memory is valid for exactly
  as long as its queue's channel lock is held, because the selector's
  post-wake node-removal pass re-locks every case channel before its
  frame can be reused or freed. Every waker therefore claims/pops under
  the channel lock and never touches a node after unlock.
- Close: observed in the ready scan (`case_ready`) and after wake.
  Receive on closed+empty fires with the zero value (matches
  jinn_chan_recv end-of-stream). Send on closed fires without sending —
  statement-send's silent-false. The frontend only generates receive
  cases today (`is_send` is always 0 in emit_select); when send cases
  arrive, jinn_select_case_t needs a per-case result slot so a fired
  send-to-closed can surface `false` to the arm, per the 8-11 DoD.
- Retry exhaustion is gone, not repaired: -1 now means default (or
  cancellation — the generated post-suspension check unwinds). A selector
  with no default and nothing ready parks indefinitely.
- No case cap: order/node/lock arrays are alloca'd from the
  codegen-static case count (old fixed [16] silently ignored cases 17+).
- The inherited combined stress (select + scope join + actor join +
  channel churn, ASan, 10-way parallel) found and fixed two adjacent
  lifetime bugs:
  1. scope.c: jinn_scope_child_done decremented live_children BEFORE
     taking the scope lock, so a running (not yet parked) parent could
     observe zero, return from join, and free the scope while the child
     was about to CAS its spinlock. The decrement now sits inside the
     lock and join confirms zero by a lock acquire+release barrier.
  2. actor.c: jinn_actor_destroy freed the mailbox channel at actor
     exit while a concurrent `stop e` had already loaded the pointer —
     close-after-free. The channel is now closed, detached, and retired
     with the mailbox (freed in jinn_actor_retire_flush at shutdown), so
     a stale stop/send only touches a closed, still-allocated channel.
- Known ASan-environment artifact, NOT a runtime bug: rare SEGV in
  libasan's __tls_get_addr interceptor during worker-thread bootstrap
  (T-1, libc start_thread), under many concurrent instrumented processes.
  Reproduced identically on the unmodified pre-8-11 runtime with a
  select-free program; frequency correlates with process-lifetime
  shortness, not with select. Excluded from the DoD sweep results.
- Sweep: 60 runs × 7 programs (combined churn, two-channel wake, close
  pre/during park, 32-case, 4-selector contention exactly-once, fairness)
  at 10-way parallelism under ASan+UBSan: zero sanitizer reports besides
  the environmental artifact above. TSan remains fiber-blind (see 8-10
  notes); the deque keeps its pthread-only TSan harness.
